# Quarkus health checks

Add `quarkus-smallrye-health`. Use the built-in MicroProfile Health endpoints. Do **not** create a JAX-RS `@Path("/health")` (or similar) resource. Do not implement liveness/readiness by hand.

Quarkus serves (non-application root `/q` by default):

| Path | Probe |
|---|---|
| `/q/health/live` | liveness |
| `/q/health/ready` | readiness (includes extension checks) |
| `/q/health/started` | startup |
| `/q/health` | all of the above |

Keep `quarkus.http.non-application-root-path` as `/q` unless the platform mandates otherwise. Do not move health onto the application root to “make Swagger show it.”

Kubernetes / GKE:

- liveness → `GET /q/health/live`
- readiness → `GET /q/health/ready`
- startup → `GET /q/health/started` when the platform supports it

```yaml
quarkus:
  smallrye-health:
    root-path: health
```

That yields `/q/health`, `/q/health/live`, `/q/health/ready`, `/q/health/started`. Do not set `root-path` to a second tree that duplicates probes.

## Extra checks

Add `@Liveness`, `@Readiness`, or `@Startup` beans **only** for checks the extensions do not already provide. Do not wrap the OOTB endpoints.

```kotlin
@ApplicationScoped
@Readiness
class DownstreamReadyCheck : HealthCheck {
    override fun call(): HealthCheckResponse =
        HealthCheckResponse.named("downstream-config").up().build()
}
```

A custom check must not call downstream HTTP on every probe if that would stampede `getPosDeviceTransaction` / `getProducts`. Config/presence checks are fine; a full downstream GET is not.
